# Docker build context for apps/worker/Dockerfile. The context is the REPO ROOT, because the
# worker is a pnpm-workspace importer that consumes packages/core and packages/db.
#
# DENY EVERYTHING, THEN RE-ADMIT THE BUILD'S INPUTS. An allow-list is used deliberately: the
# deny-list version of this file shipped a 1.37 GB context, because untracked build output
# nobody thinks about — apps/macos/.build (SwiftPM, 600 MB), apps/desktop/src-tauri/target
# (Rust, 2.4 GB) — grows faster than anyone maintains exclusions. With an allow-list the
# context is ~2 MB and a NEW kind of junk cannot silently join it.
*

# Workspace manifests. Every importer's package.json must be present or `pnpm install
# --frozen-lockfile` refuses: it validates the lockfile against the importers it finds.
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml
!tsconfig.json
!tsconfig.base.json
!apps/*/package.json
!packages/*/package.json

# The four projects that are actually compiled and shipped. `packages/db-mail` joined when
# the engine/cloud split made it a real workspace package: the db package's tsconfig
# references it, so `tsc -b` inside the image fails with TS5083 without its sources — which is
# exactly how the first post-split worker image build died. The allow-list
# admitted its package.json (workspace manifest) but not the package, so the failure only
# appeared at image build time, never locally.
#
# THE STANDING RULE, and read it as a rule rather than as that one deploy's history: ANY new
# workspace package the worker compiles against needs a line here, and nothing local can tell you
# it is missing, because a laptop has the whole repository.
#
# ITS OTHER EDGE, which cost a second deploy: a line here is also the only thing that CAN admit a
# package the worker must not have, and adding one is the last step of a violation rather than the
# fix for one. `packages/services` was admitted for a day for exactly that reason — the worker's
# maintenance slot ran the `attachment_staging` retention sweep, so `apps/worker/src/index.ts`
# imported it, the worker's tsconfig referenced it, and the image build died on TS5083
# (cannot read the services package's tsconfig) until this list let it in. It built
# after that, and it also put an HTML sanitiser and its parser in the worker's boot graph, which
# on Node 23 is a hard ERR_REQUIRE_CYCLE_MODULE at import time; only the Dockerfile's pinned
# Node 22 kept the container starting. The sweep moved down to `@trafficflow/db/cloud` instead and
# the line came back out. The `!packages/…` lines below are exactly the TRANSITIVE project-
# reference closure of the worker's tsconfig — core and db because the worker names them,
# db-mail because db does — and a workspace test computes that closure and fails if
# this list disagrees with it or with `package.json`'s dependencies. If you are about to add a
# name none of those three produce, the import that wants it is the thing to move.
!apps/worker
!packages/core
!packages/db
!packages/db-mail

# …minus anything inside them that must never be copied in. `dist/` and `*.tsbuildinfo` are
# the important pair: the worker's CLI boots from dist/, dist/ is gitignored, and a laptop
# holding a stale dist/ plus a matching .tsbuildinfo makes `tsc -b` decide there is nothing
# to emit — which is exactly how a smoke test once "passed" against an artifact nobody had
# rebuilt. Keeping both out means the image ALWAYS compiles from source. `test` is out
# because nothing in the image compiles or runs a suite — a runtime layer carrying test
# trees is bloat at best and a place for stray fixtures to ship at worst.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/test
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.DS_Store
**/.claude

# Secrets never enter an image layer; every value arrives as a Railway service variable.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
